1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,777
<font color="#000000">From NVIDIA Research at CVPR 2023,</font>

2
00:00:02,857 --> 00:00:07,283
<font color="#000000">this work closes a safety-critical gap in object
pose estimation.</font>

3
00:00:07,363 --> 00:00:12,412
<font color="#000000">Standard two-stage pipelines detect keypoints, then
solve for the 6D pose,</font>

4
00:00:12,492 --> 00:00:16,225
<font color="#000000">but give no guarantee on how wrong the answer might be.</font>

5
00:00:16,305 --> 00:00:18,097
<font color="#000000">The authors add two pieces.</font>

6
00:00:18,177 --> 00:00:24,474
<font color="#000000">Conformal keypoint detection turns heatmap
detections into circular or elliptical prediction</font>

7
00:00:24,554 --> 00:00:29,326
<font color="#000000">sets that provably cover the true keypoints with a
chosen probability,</font>

8
00:00:29,406 --> 00:00:30,643
<font color="#000000">say ninety percent.</font>

9
00:00:30,723 --> 00:00:37,853
<font color="#000000">Geometric uncertainty propagation pushes those sets
through the geometry to form a Pose Uncertainty Set,</font>

10
00:00:37,933 --> 00:00:40,140
<font color="#000000">or PURSE, covering the true pose.</font>

11
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:47,211
<font color="#000000">From PURSE they compute an average pose and use
semidefinite relaxation to bound the worst-case error.</font>

12
00:00:47,291 --> 00:00:51,370
<font color="#000000">On LineMOD Occlusion, coverage is valid and the
bounds hold.</font>

13
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:56,455
<font color="#000000">Estimating an object's 6D pose from an image
underlies augmented reality,</font>

14
00:00:56,535 --> 00:01:00,454
<font color="#000000">autonomous driving, robotic manipulation, and space
robotics.</font>

15
00:01:00,534 --> 00:01:04,846
<font color="#000000">The dominant recipe is two stages: first detect
semantic keypoints,</font>

16
00:01:04,926 --> 00:01:10,352
<font color="#000000">then recover the pose by minimizing reprojection
error through Perspective-n-Points.</font>

17
00:01:10,432 --> 00:01:12,908
<font color="#000000">These methods score well on benchmarks,</font>

18
00:01:12,988 --> 00:01:17,890
<font color="#000000">yet they return a single pose with no statement
about how trustworthy it is.</font>

19
00:01:17,970 --> 00:01:23,200
<font color="#000000">For safety-critical systems that is a serious gap:
no guarantee on the keypoints,</font>

20
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:26,346
<font color="#000000">no guarantee the optimizer found the right pose,</font>

21
00:01:26,426 --> 00:01:30,410
<font color="#000000">and no formal bound on the worst-case error against
the truth.</font>

22
00:01:30,790 --> 00:01:31,921
<font color="#000000">Why is this hard?</font>

23
00:01:32,001 --> 00:01:34,342
<font color="#000000">The authors name three challenges.</font>

24
00:01:34,422 --> 00:01:39,896
<font color="#000000">First, neural keypoint detectors can be arbitrarily
wrong, producing outliers.</font>

25
00:01:39,976 --> 00:01:44,240
<font color="#000000">Second, rejecting those outliers is a nonconvex
optimization;</font>

26
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:51,360
<font color="#000000">fast heuristics like RANSAC are common but cannot
guarantee global optimality and can fail silently.</font>

27
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:57,413
<font color="#000000">Third, and most fundamental, there is no provably
correct uncertainty quantification,</font>

28
00:01:57,493 --> 00:02:02,113
<font color="#000000">no formal worst-case error bound between estimate
and groundtruth.</font>

29
00:02:02,193 --> 00:02:09,804
<font color="#000000">Certifying that the optimizer is globally optimal
still gives no probabilistic guarantee on the pose itself.</font>

30
00:02:09,884 --> 00:02:13,720
<font color="#000000">This paper delivers exactly that guarantee, end to end.</font>

31
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:15,778
<font color="#000000">The method has two stages.</font>

32
00:02:15,858 --> 00:02:17,942
<font color="#000000">In conformal keypoint detection,</font>

33
00:02:18,022 --> 00:02:23,893
<font color="#000000">a calibration set of labeled images gives
nonconformity scores for the heatmap detector.</font>

34
00:02:23,973 --> 00:02:26,463
<font color="#000000">Two designs are offered: a peak score,</font>

35
00:02:26,543 --> 00:02:31,197
<font color="#000000">the scaled distance from the true keypoint to the
most probable pixel,</font>

36
00:02:31,277 --> 00:02:35,727
<font color="#000000">and a covariance score, a Mahalanobis distance over
top detections,</font>

37
00:02:35,807 --> 00:02:38,297
<font color="#000000">yielding circular and elliptical sets.</font>

38
00:02:38,377 --> 00:02:45,736
<font color="#000000">Sorting the scores gives a threshold so each true
keypoint lies in its set with probability one minus epsilon.</font>

39
00:02:45,816 --> 00:02:48,238
<font color="#000000">In geometric uncertainty propagation,</font>

40
00:02:48,318 --> 00:02:51,754
<font color="#000000">these sets become quadratic constraints on the pose,</font>

41
00:02:51,834 --> 00:02:54,054
<font color="#000000">defining the Pose Uncertainty Set.</font>

42
00:02:54,134 --> 00:02:57,705
<font color="#000000">Since it is nonconvex, RANSAG samples three keypoints,</font>

43
00:02:57,785 --> 00:03:02,980
<font color="#000000">solves the minimal P3P problem, keeps poses inside
the set, and averages them.</font>

44
00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:07,917
<font color="#000000">Finally, maximizing the distance to the farthest
pose still inside PURSE,</font>

45
00:03:07,997 --> 00:03:10,216
<font color="#000000">relaxed as a semidefinite program,</font>

46
00:03:10,296 --> 00:03:13,800
<font color="#000000">certifies worst-case rotation and translation bounds.</font>

47
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:17,933
<font color="#000000">The results confirm the theory on three fronts.</font>

48
00:03:18,013 --> 00:03:21,766
<font color="#000000">First, coverage is valid: at a ninety percent target,</font>

49
00:03:21,846 --> 00:03:25,599
<font color="#000000">empirical coverage across objects sits right around
ninety percent and tightens as the calibration set grows.</font>

50
00:03:25,679 --> 00:03:29,432
<font color="#000000">Second,</font>

51
00:03:29,512 --> 00:03:33,265
<font color="#000000">the bounds are correct: in scatter plots of
certified bound versus actual error,</font>

52
00:03:33,345 --> 00:03:37,098
<font color="#000000">every case where PURSE covers the true pose stays
below the diagonal,</font>

53
00:03:37,178 --> 00:03:40,931
<font color="#000000">so the bound is never violated when the guarantee holds.</font>

54
00:03:41,011 --> 00:03:44,764
<font color="#000000">Third, the average pose is accurate,</font>

55
00:03:44,844 --> 00:03:48,597
<font color="#000000">beating prior methods on the two-dimensional
projection metric with groundtruth boxes</font>

56
00:03:48,677 --> 00:03:52,430
<font color="#000000">and staying comparable with Faster-RCNN detections.</font>

57
00:03:52,810 --> 00:04:00,086
<font color="#000000">The lasting message: you can turn a standard
two-stage pose estimator into one that tells you how wrong it might be,</font>

58
00:04:00,166 --> 00:04:04,017
<font color="#000000">with a real statistical guarantee, and pay little
in accuracy.</font>

59
00:04:04,097 --> 00:04:07,568
<font color="#000000">Conformal prediction supplies coverage on the keypoints,</font>

60
00:04:07,648 --> 00:04:10,358
<font color="#000000">geometry propagates it to the pose as PURSE,</font>

61
00:04:10,438 --> 00:04:15,240
<font color="#000000">and semidefinite relaxation converts the set into
concrete worst-case bounds.</font>

62
00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:18,221
<font color="#000000">Because it wraps any heatmap keypoint detector,</font>

63
00:04:18,301 --> 00:04:23,230
<font color="#000000">the approach points toward provably correct
perception wherever safety matters.</font>
